README.DOC:

The unpacked files from the Distribution Diskette in this release of 
the Host Software for the COP8 Evaluation & Programming Unit (EPU) are:

    README.DOC     The file you are now reading.

      === Host Software Files ===

    EPU880.BAT     Batch file used to invoke the EPU-COP8 Host Software
                   executable program when using a COP880 EPU.
    EPU888GG.BAT   Batch file used to invoke the EPU-COP8 Host Software
                   executable program when using a COP888GG EPU.
    EPU888FH.BAT   Batch file used to invoke the EPU-COP8 Host Software
                   executable program when using a COP888FH EPU.

                   (To determine what model of EPU you have, look at the
                    COP8 chip that is located next to the 40-pin header
                    (J1).  Every EPU board has two COP8 devices.  The one
                    that determines the model of the EPU board is the COP8
                    chip located next to the 40-pin header (J1).)

    EPU.EXE        EPU-COP8 Host Software executable program.
                   (Do not invoke this program directly;
                    rather, use one of the EPU*.BAT files listed above.)

    $CLR1$         File containing default video display color and 
                   highlighting values suitable for color CGA/EGA/VGA monitors.

    $CLR2$         File containing default video display color and 
                   highlighting values suitable for color EGA/VGA monitors.

    $CLRL$         File containing default video display "color" and 
                   highlighting values suitable for LCD monitors.

    $CLRM$         File containing default video display "color" and 
                   highlighting values suitable for monochrome monitors.

    $EPU_MOD.880   Host Sofware configuration file for the COP880   processor.
    $EPU_MOD.8GG    "      "          "        "    "   "  COP888GG     "     
    $EPU_MOD.8FH    "      "          "        "    "   "  COP888FH     "     

    $EPU_HLP       Host Sofware On-line Help support files.
    $EPU_HLI        


      === Demonstration Programs ===

      --- C8A1.COF:  Assembly Language Demo Program ---
      --- (National COP8 Assembler/Linker)          ---

    C8A1.COF      Linker-generated absolute, loadable COFF file
                  (with debug information)
    C8A1MAKE.BAT  Batch file to create loadable C8A1.COF file from scratch
    C8A1MAKE.LNK  Linker control file for creating C8A1.COF
    C8A1INC.INC   COP8 demo program, Include File (COP8 assembly source)
    C8A1MAIN.ASM  COP8 demo program, Module #1    (COP8 assembly source)
    C8A1SPIN.ASM  COP8 demo program, Module #2    (COP8 assembly source)

      --- BC82MAIN.COD: Byte Craft C Demo Program ---

    BC82INNR.C    Function 'innerloop()' (C source)
    BC82MAIN.C    Main program (function 'main()';  C source)
    BC82MAIN.COD  Loadable, absolute object module format,
                  (with debug information)
    BC82MAIN.HEX  Loadable HEX file for BC82MAIN
    BC82MAIN.SYM  Compiler-generated symbolic information file for BC82MAIN
    BC82MAKE.BAT  Batch file to generate BC82MAIN.COD from scratch
    BC82PRAG.H    C #include file
    BC82WAST.C    Function 'wastetime()' (C source)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Beginning with Version 3.0 Revision 12, the host software utilizes not only
code overlaying, but also data overlaying.  This capability is provided by the
'.RTLink/Plus' linker (.RTLink/Plus is a trademark of Pocket Soft, Inc.).
The advantage of overlaying data as well as code is that the host software
consumes less memory during execution.  This provides more memory for you
to load larger programs with full symbolic information.

During execution of the host software, the .RTLink/Plus overlay manager swaps
overlay sections out to temporary files on disk.  If you have the environment
variable 'TMP' defined, .RTLink/Plus uses the directory designated by this
variable.  If the 'TMP' environment variable is not defined, .RTLink/Plus
places the temporary files in the root directory.  In either case, the
temporary files all have the extension '.TMP'.

If, during execution of the host software, there is not enough available
disk space for .RTLink/Plus to create one of the temporary files,
you will see the messages:

     eca0001: .RTLink CACHE - Save File Handling
     DOS Function code 0040, Error code FFFF

In some cases, the first line above will not be displayed and you will see
only the 'DOS Function code' message.  In either case, the message(s) may
display anywhere on the screen.  When this happens, the host software has
been terminated and you may even need to reboot your computer.  Delete any
residual '.TMP' files and ensure that you have enough available disk space.
We have found that approximately 400K-800K of disk space should be free
before invoking the host software.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Software Setup:  Environment Variables 'TMP' and 'TEMP':

The 'TMP' and 'TEMP' environment variables are used not only by the host
software program, but also by many other programs.  Each designates the
directory where an executing program's temporary files should be created.
These temporary files typically are for the program's internal use and are
usually deleted when the program terminates.  To determine where these
temporary files should be created, some programs use the environment
variable 'TMP', others use the environment variable 'TEMP'.  It is safest
and easiest to set both environment variables, 'TMP' and 'TEMP', and have
them designate ("point to") the same directory on your computer.  You can
do this by adding the following statements to your AUTOEXEC.BAT file:

      SET TEMP=C:\TMP
      SET  TMP=C:\TMP

It is important that the designated directory ('C:\TMP' in this case)
actually exists on your computer.  You can create this directory by
typing:

      MKDIR C:\TMP

If the designated directory does not exist on your computer, the host
software will probably terminate with the following message displayed
somewhere on the screen:

     Fatal Error evm0013:  Error in VM save file handling
                           (Probably out of disk space).

If you choose to have separate directories for 'TMP' and 'TEMP', you
would add something like the following to your AUTOEXEC.BAT file:

      SET TEMP=C:\ABC
      SET  TMP=C:\XYZ\ZYX

and type the following to create the designated directories:

      MKDIR C:\ABC
      MKDIR C:\XYZ
      MKDIR C:\XYZ\ZYX

Note that the actual directory path(s) and name(s) can be whatever you
choose.  The only critical items are the names of the environment
variables ('TMP' and 'TEMP') set to designate those directory path(s)
and name(s).

In a networked environment, make sure that you have "create" and "write"
permissions on the drive and directory that you designate for both 'TMP'
and 'TEMP'.

=== End of:  README.DOC ===
